  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35933</id>
    <title>Losing Their Religion</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Richard Murphy &amp; Shqiponja Telhaj</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;This paper studies whether governments can change social norms that constrain women’s education and work, and through which mechanism. Albania’s 1967 religion ban, which created the world’s first atheist state, provides an extreme natural experiment: religious practice was outlawed nationwide, but a 1948 classification of historic monuments determined which religious buildings remained standing. We find larger increases in female employment and schooling where no monument survived. The public visibility of the old religious order appears to be the mechanism: effects are largest where no monument remained, smaller with rural-only monuments, and smallest with urban monuments; they also decline as monument count rises, regardless of denomination. We establish how social norms can outlive formal authority.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35933"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35934</id>
    <title>Tax Productivity and Economic Development: A Quantitative Macroeconomic Analysis</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Radhika Goyal, Anders Jensen, David Lagakos, Abdoulaye Ndiaye &amp; Yuntian Zhao</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;This paper examines the role of government capacity to collect taxes in driving economic development. We first draw on a new macro-historical database to document that tax collections relative to tax administration expenditures rise sharply with GDP per capita. We then interpret this finding in a neoclassical growth model with a tax administration sector in which labor inputs are required to raise tax revenues. Countries differ in their levels of tax productivity, capturing how effectively labor is used to raise taxes, as well as their levels of productivity in creating consumption and investment goods. Governments use tax revenues to provide public capital that complements private factors in production and to make transfers. Quantitatively, our model predicts that increases in tax productivity in low-income economies can raise GDP per capita substantially more than suggested by Hulten’s theorem.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35934"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35930</id>
    <title>College Success: Understanding the Roles of Financial Aid, Loans, Preparation, and Incentives</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Douglas N. Harris, Christopher R. Taber &amp; Yu (Alan) Yang</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Over the past four decades, the earnings gap between college graduates and non-graduates has widened substantially, prompting interest in policies aimed at promoting higher education among low-income students. A key challenge for these policies is the high college dropout rate. This paper develops a dynamic model of college enrollment and completion with rich individual heterogeneity to assess how various factors influence college attendance, dropout, and graduation. We estimate the model using data from longitudinal surveys, results of quasi-experimental studies on grant aid, and large-scale randomized controlled trials. We use the model to simulate the effects of tuition subsidies, college loans, financial incentives, and increased pre-college skills on attendance and completion. We find moderate effects of most unconditional financial policies on attendance, with small effects on completion conditional on attendance. By contrast, some merit-based incentives, such as forgiving student loans upon receipt of a bachelor’s degree, have substantial effects. However, this type of policy and other financial subsidies accrue even more than the others to students from relatively wealthy backgrounds. Overall, it appears infeasible to design a financial aid policy that is simultaneously effective in raising attainment and fiscally progressive. By comparison, we find that the greatest potential for increasing attainment could come from better preparing students for college.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35930"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35953</id>
    <title>Competition in Generative AI: Evidence from Consumer Demand</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Mark L. Egan, Dominic Russel, Claire Shi &amp; Vincent Yao</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;How competitive will the market for generative AI be as it matures? Using novel transaction level credit and debit card data covering 174 million consumers and 114 billion transactions, we study the adoption of paid services and the demand-side foundations of competition in this market. As of April 2026, approximately 2.9% of U.S. consumers pay for at least one major generative AI service, with adoption highest among younger and higher-income consumers. Nearly all of these paid users subscribe to basic plans that cost $30 or less per month. Three patterns suggest limited differentiation and low switching costs. First, although multihoming is common, it has declined as providers’ capabilities have expanded. Second, annual customer retention rates range from 20% to 45% across major AI providers, lower than those for many other subscription products. Third, switching rises around major model releases. Motivated by these findings, we develop and estimate a structural model of AI service demand and supply that reveals strong competitive pressure. Consumers are highly price sensitive, have much weaker preferences for particular providers than for AI overall, and view AI services as increasingly close substitutes. Our results suggest that, absent a substantial increase in AI model quality differentiation, growing substitutability among services will constrain providers’ pricing power and allow consumers to capture a large share of the surplus generated by these services.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35953"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35788</id>
    <title>Beliefs Over Contracts</title>
    <updated>2026-10-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Francis Annan &amp; Collin B. Raymond</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We study how firms choose among incentive contracts and how accurately managers predict their effects. Using a survey of managerial beliefs and a large-scale field experiment, we randomly assign agents at the market level to several widely used, expenditure-equivalent incentive schemes. In the field, the best-performing contract raises weekly firm profit by over 55% relative to the status quo, despite being ranked lower by managers, whereas the worst-performing contract performs as predicted. Managers reliably avoid poorly performing contracts but systematically underestimate top-performing ones. Standard contract theory does better as a forecaster here: an unbiased textbook principal orders the contracts correctly, or very nearly so, correctly identifying the profit leader where managers do not; where it does miss, the error is similar to managers’ and for a similar reason. We show that the performance differences across contracts reflect a mix of labor-supply and risk responses, and that managerial predictability varies with contract complexity and managerial hierarchy. Our results highlight the importance of contract design for firm performance and reveal systematic limits to managerial cognition in shaping incentives.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35788"/>
