  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35981</id>
    <title>Beyond the Numbers: Professional Forecasters’ Narratives about Inflation and Stock Market Performance</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Christian Conrad, Julius Theodor Schoelkopf, Michael Weber &amp; Frank Brueckbauer</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Professional forecasters' heterogeneous narratives about how inflation affects stock markets in 2023 rationalize their high disagreement regarding quantitative expectations for 12-month-ahead inflation and stock returns in December 2022. Professional forecasters causally update their return expectations in heterogeneous directions in response to information about the inflation outlook, depending on the narrative they entertain. Moreover, the narratives also affect their asset allocation in a hypothetical portfolio-choice experiment. Providing common signals does not necessarily lead to convergence of beliefs if agents subscribe to different narratives.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35981"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
