  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35991</id>
    <title>Doing Better at Doing Good: Lessons from a Bayesian Meta-Analysis After 20 Years of Poverty Graduation Programs</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Samantha Carter, Edward Jee, Dean Karlan &amp; Patrick Premand</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Multifaceted poverty Graduation programs that aim to increase income generating activities for low-income households are being rapidly adopted and scaled by nonprofit organizations and governments. This paper conducts a Bayesian hierarchical meta-analysis of 49 studies (encompassing 81 treatment variations) on poverty Graduation programs, defined as containing at least three of five components: consumption support, savings facilitation, skills training, coaching, and a lumpsum transfer. The analysis finds large gains across economic and psychosocial outcomes: monthly consumption rises by 38 USD PPP (11 percent), income by 28 USD PPP (15 percent), alongside improvements in savings, assets, food security, mental health, and women’s empowerment. Beyond these average treatment effects, three results stand out. First, programs with large transfers (at least 400 USD PPP) have larger treatment effects but are not more cost-effective. Second, more expensive programs generate larger treatment effects, but the improvements are not commensurate with the cost increases; thus, lower cost programs tend to be more cost-effective. Third, programs implemented from 2016 onward perform considerably better than those implemented prior to 2016: benefit-cost ratios from 2016 onward are 5.39, compared to 2.89 through 2015. Neither changes in cost nor in high-level design (components) explain this upward trend. Considering other heavily studied interventions, the results show a similar upward trend for treatment effects on behavior change from financial education but not for unconditional cash transfers. The paper hypothesizes a mechanism of continuous sector-community learning and improved implementation quality.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35991"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w35989</id>
    <title>Clearing the Fog: Price Transparency, Market Churn, and Consumer Welfare in Short-Term Rentals</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Ginger Zhe Jin, Liad Wagman &amp; Jingyi Xing</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We study California's short-term lodging price transparency law (AB 537), which from July 1, 2024 required Airbnb and other providers to advertise fee-inclusive prices. Using Coarsened Exact Matching to compare Airbnb listings in San Diego and San Francisco with those in Seattle and Chicago, we find that all-in pricing made guests more price-sensitive relative to control cities; yet, contrary to the standard prediction that transparency lowers prices, average daily rates rose by 4.8 percent. We also observe significant market churn: relative to control cities, treated cities saw more exits and entries, listings reliant on cleaning-fee revenue disproportionately exited, higher-quality listings were more likely to stay, and entrants offered lower fees and lower quality than incumbents. A discrete-choice welfare analysis shows that the law removed a sizable “welfare illusion” created by obscured fees and, largely through market churn, raised realized consumer surplus. The net gains, after accounting for price increases, were positive on average but unevenly distributed across treated ZIP codes.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w35989"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36000</id>
    <title>When More Prenatal Care Looks Like Less: Selection, Information and Maternal and Infant Health</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Chien-Tzu Cheng, Shin-Yi Chou &amp; Hsien-Ming Lien</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We use Taiwan's 2003 SARS epidemic as a natural experiment, instrumenting prenatal care with gestational exposure to SARS-induced fear that reduced visits by over 20 percent. Adequate prenatal care reduces preventable maternal complications by 3.4 percentage points (26 percent), with no effect on infant outcomes. OLS estimates are perversely signed for maternal complications but overstate benefits for infant outcomes, consistent with competing patient- and provider-driven selection.  Mechanism evidence points to prenatal care as a longitudinal information system: repeated contact facilitates detection of evolving risks and generates clinical information that informs delivery decisions, including delivery-mode routing and subsequent management at delivery.</content>
    <link href="https://www.nber.org/papers/w36000"/>
    <category term="NBER"/>
    <published>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://www.nber.org/papers/w36001</id>
    <title>Vigilance, Risk Vulnerability, and the Precautionary Augment</title>
    <updated>2026-11-01T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Miles S. Kimball &amp; Vincent A. Mastantuno</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt; NBER Working Papers&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Risk raises the expected marginal utility of a prudent agent. We define this proportional change as the precautionary augment, which measures risk-induced changes in the marginal value of saving, the Lucas Tree risk-free rate, and asset prices in a derivative-saturated market where market-state securities provide incomplete coverage against idiosyncratic risk. One agent is more vigilant than another if their precautionary augment is larger for all unfair risks. We derive and compare conditions for greater vigilance, showing how to implement the Diffidence Theorem geometrically. Because risk vulnerability is equivalent to decreasing vigilance, classic results about risk vulnerability emerge as corollaries.</content>
